WEBVTT

00:00:00.127 --> 00:00:03.000
LLM API performance has several layers.

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:06.255
Prompt processing latency is the time spent reading the input context.

00:00:06.255 --> 00:00:08.987
Generation speed is often measured in tokens per second.

00:00:08.987 --> 00:00:12.000
Queueing delay appears when more requests arrive than the service can handle immediately.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:15.080
User experience depends on the combination of these factors,

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:16.995
not a single benchmark number.

00:00:16.995 --> 00:00:21.080
Context windows affect serving performance because longer prompts consume more memory and

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:21.995
increase prompt processing time.

00:00:21.995 --> 00:00:25.963
Concurrency increases pressure on CPU, GPU, RAM, and VRAM.

00:00:25.963 --> 00:00:29.000
Request size limits help prevent oversized prompts from overwhelming the service.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:32.085
Batch settings may improve throughput in some runtimes,

00:00:32.085 --> 00:00:34.250
but they can also increase latency or memory use.

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:37.000
Performance tuning should be based on real workloads.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:41.400
Reliability practices include health checks, readiness checks, graceful failure, timeouts,

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:44.000
cancellation, clear error responses, and fallback behavior.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:47.410
A health check should confirm that the service is available and able to respond as

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:48.000
expected,

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:49.980
not merely that a process exists.

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:54.995
Fallback behavior might include a smaller model, a queued response, a retry policy,

00:00:54.995 --> 00:00:57.000
or a clear message that the service is unavailable.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:58.955
Scaling can be simple or complex.

00:00:58.955 --> 00:01:02.000
A small private API may need only one machine and conservative limits.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:06.655
A production service may need autoscaling, queue management, model warmup, load balancing,

00:01:06.655 --> 00:01:07.980
and capacity planning.

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:10.970
The engineering challenge is balancing throughput, cost,

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:13.540
and user experience while preserving privacy, reliability,

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:15.000
and clear operational boundaries.
